## 6. Performance Considerations

Calls to C functions from Zig have the same cost as calls from C: a direct jump with the standard ABI overhead. There is no trampoline, marshalling layer, or runtime translation. The inline potential is limited because Zig’s optimizer does not inline across `extern` boundaries unless link-time optimization (LTO) is enabled. To enable LTO for C libraries compiled by Zig, use `-flto` and ensure the C code is compiled with `zig cc` or with `-flto` flags. The standard `@cImport` mechanism only provides declarations, not definitions; the actual library code is compiled separately.

The compile-time cost of processing large headers can be substantial. For example, `windows.h` generates tens of thousands of declarations. The self-hosted C translator is improving but may still be slower than `bindgen` for certain edge cases. Mitigation: use a cache file or pre-generate bindings with `zig translate-c` and commit the output as a Zig file. The `std.os.windows` module does exactly this—it is a pre-generated binding, not live `@cImport`.

When calling C functions that expect arrays or buffers, avoid unnecessary copies. Zig slices and C arrays share the same memory layout, so you can pass `.ptr` and `.len` where a C function expects a pointer and a length parameter. For functions that expect a null-terminated string, you must ensure the zero-termination, which might require allocating an extra byte if the slice is not already C-compatible. That allocation overhead can be eliminated by storing the data in a `[:0]const u8` from the start.

Inlining small C helper functions or macros manually in Zig can reduce call overhead, especially for repeated operations inside hot loops. Use comptime to generate typed wrappers that avoid the C type conversion thunks—Zig can optimize the wrapper entirely.

